From cb6c33d257f74718bd72341c3b4f4de12ffa1cf5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jun Kawasaki <jun784@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 01:17:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] effective: the grant is addressed, and the address is checked
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`requested ∩ delegated ∩ local-policy` decided what may be reached and never
who by. The datum was not missing — `:cap/holder` is one of `:identity-keys`
in the language's semantics — but the answer was copied out of the request:

    :cap/holder (:cap/holder requested)

A caller stating `:cap/holder "did:key:zWhoever"` had that string arrive
intact in the capability handed to the handler, compared to nothing. The
claim and the check were on the same side of the boundary.

- `biscuit.kotoba/->delegated` now reads `[holder <principal>]`, the one fact
  whose omission did not fail toward less authority: dropping it turned an
  addressed token back into a bearer token.
- `biscuit.effective/authorize` takes `:holder` — the authenticated principal,
  the sealed output of an authentication that already happened — and drops
  `(:cap/holder requested)` on entry rather than propagating it. An addressed
  grant is usable only by its addressee (`:wrong-holder`, `:holder-required`);
  an unaddressed one is a bearer grant that says so, and
  `:policy/require-holder true` declines it, the same shape
  `:policy/forbid-wildcard` already uses.
- The effective holder is the LAST holder fact, not the first, in both this
  fold and `biscuit.authority/->grant`. `authority.grant/meet` states the
  rule: the holder is the child's, a delegation ends at whoever holds the
  leaf. Taking the first made a later block's re-addressing a silent no-op —
  the parent kept the token and the child got nothing.
- Every receipt, allowed and denied alike, carries `:receipt/holder` and
  `:receipt/holder-binding`, and `:unauthenticated` is a distinct value from
  `:bearer`. Extra keys, not additions to `receipt-keys`:
  `:receipt-requirements` is the floor the language sets, and a floor is not
  a ceiling.

No new decider. `authority/grant.cljc` already compares a holder when a grant
names one; this puts the same question on the capability-semantics path,
which had no holder check at all.

Measured with nbb (the JVM `:test` alias needs a JDK toolchain not present in
this environment): 77 tests / 202 assertions / 0 failures, from a 71/172
baseline. Each change was shown to discriminate — reverting the request
passthrough reddens 2 tests, reverting last-to-first reddens 2 here and 3 in
the authority fold, and un-reading the holder fact reddens 5.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
